DISTRIBUTION OF SIGNED PER-ROW DRIFT   (exact rows only)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  positive (over-attached)   72
  negative (under-attached)  103
  exactly zero               28,475   (32 of them WRONG postings — see the blind spot below)

  positive magnitudes  n=72  min 0.02  median 800.00K  p90 4.00M  p99 119.54M  max 358.73M  sum 476.03M
  negative magnitudes  n=103  min 0.38  median 21.83M  p90 117.52M  p99 200.00M  max 358.73M  sum 4.49B

  direction-normalised (drift x sign of the statement amount), 175 non-zero rows:
    over-attached in the row's own direction   44
    under-attached                             131
    net normalised                             -3.42B
    net / gross, normalised                   68.821%
      Compare this with the raw net/gross above. The raw figure treats a
      statement debit and a statement credit as opposite, so the two cancel
      in the total even when both are errors in the same direction. Where
      the normalised ratio is the higher of the two, the bias is systematic
      and the raw net understates it.

    by label stratum:
      multi-key    over     1   under   112   net       -3.82B
      blank        over    34   under     0   net      766.42M
      single-key   over     9   under    19   net     -360.19M
      A multi-key label cannot be answered by an auto-close, which posts
      exactly one key, and 112 of 113 such rows under-attach (99.115%).
      Not all of them: one large ledger row can still exceed the sum of
      several small ones, so the direction is empirical, not structural.
      Blank labels over-attach without exception, and that one IS structural:
      the label names no ledger value at all, so drift is the whole posted
      amount, which matched the statement to the cent.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BY EXCEPTION CLASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  NOT COMPUTABLE. exception_class is empty on all 28,915 auto-closed rows,
  and 28,915 of them recorded no trigger either.

  This is by construction, not a gap in the audit. In controller.py the escalate
  flag is  esc = t_no | t_add | t_fee | t_low,  and the class is a select over
  five branches built from those same four triggers. A row carries a class
  exactly when a trigger fires, and escalates exactly when a trigger fires. An
  auto-closed row is therefore, by definition, a row with no class. Classes
  partition the escalated queue; they do not reach the auto-closed population,
  and no breakdown of auto-close drift by exception class exists to be reported.

  Substituted below are the two axes that DO vary across auto-closed rows: the
  stratum of the label, and the one class-adjacent flag the audit records for
  rows that were not escalated.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
