WORKED EXAMPLE — the >= 100M bucket, every wrong posting in it
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  B_id                      |B amount|               drift  label
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  535742045803                 949.00M                0.00  single-key
  132562348380                 358.73M             358.73M  blank
  234837519256                 358.73M            -358.73M  blank

  net of the exact ones   0.00
  gross                   717.46M
  value mis-attributed    1.67B   (exposure.py's measure, all rows above)

  The net is exactly zero and the postings are all wrong. Opposite-signed
  errors of equal magnitude cancel, and a wrong posting onto an
  equal-valued key contributes nothing to begin with. A balance check over
  this bucket would report that nothing had happened.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS MEASUREMENT IS BLIND TO
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  wrong postings with drift EXACTLY zero        32 of 207 exact wrong rows (15.459%)
  wrong postings whose interval straddles zero  128 of 265 bounded wrong rows

  value mis-attributed by those zero-drift rows  1.04B
    — invisible to any balance check, because the wrong key carries the same
      amount as the right one. The posting is wrong; the totals agree.

  This is the repeat regime showing up in the drift figures. Where several
  ledger rows already carry the statement's exact amount, choosing the wrong
  one produces zero drift by construction. A net-drift check cannot find those
  errors, and on this data they are not a rounding detail.

  Also outside this measurement, by construction:
    * escalated rows. Drift is defined over postings the system made without
      review; an escalated row has not been posted, so it has no drift.
    * every auto-closed row matched its top candidate to within 1 cent
      (controller.py escalates on t_fee = has & ~exact_top1, TOL_CENTS = 1), so
      the posted side of every row here is the statement amount to the cent.
      Drift is entirely a property of what the LABEL says, never of a
      near-miss on the amount.

